 "brief": {
  "tone": "Brisk, witty Victorian detective mystery with a touch of comedy at Holmes's expense and a sense of royal danger tempered by fond irony.",
  "depiction_rules": [
   "Holmes is shown with his analytical, observant manner and disguises (groom, clergyman) rendered convincingly; no cocaine use should be shown explicitly.",
   "The King is shown masked in his first appearance, unmasked only after he tears the mask off in the room.",
   "Irene Adler is glimpsed rather than fully revealed in the early sections, presented as beautiful and resolute; her face is seen clearly only at the threshold and in the photograph.",
   "The street quarrel and smoke-rocket fire alarm are shown as staged comedy, not real violence; Holmes's blood is red paint."
  ],
  "era": {
   "name": "London and Bohemia, late 1880s (March 1888)",
   "people": "English gentlemen in frock and tweed; a Bohemian King of Teutonic bearing; London street types such as cabmen, ostlers, a scissors-grinder, guardsmen and loungers; servants in plain dress.",
   "dress": "Victorian: frock coats, top hats, ulsters, walking dress and bonnets for women; the King wears rich astrakhan-trimmed double-breasted coat, a blue cloak lined with flame-coloured silk and a beryl brooch, with fur-trimmed boots.",
   "buildings_and_objects": "Gaslit London with lamplighting at dusk; hansom cabs, broughams and landaus drawn by horses; villas with sliding panels and window fasteners; cabinet-size photographs; pink notepaper watermarked with Bohemian paper-mill marks; a gasogene and spirit case.",
   "technology": "Pre-electric lighting (gas and oil lamps), railway travel (the 5:15 train from Charing Cross), telegraph wires, smoke-rockets and plumber's tools; no telephones or motor cars in use here."
  }
